  "summary": "Preprints and publications this period add validation, lineage, and throughput as explicit software concerns for multi-omics workflows.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is software infrastructure for omics data rather than new biological findings. COSMOS pairs automated validation and SQL warehousing with R Shiny access for FAIR- and GCP-aligned clinical trial omics data, while OmicsLake and bMINTY address traceability through dataset-level lineage tracking and RO-Crate metadata export. Reproducibility and scale recur in pipeline-oriented tools: Utanos is a modular Nextflow workflow for shallow whole-genome sequencing, and the PMBB Geno-Pheno Toolkit offers containerized pipelines for cross-biobank association analyses. fastCDS, reportedly two to three orders of magnitude faster than its comparison, maps protein domains to genomic coordinates. IOBRpy, pyfraglib, and scGraphVerse extend this pattern to immuno-oncology, cfDNA fragmentomics, and single-cell gene network inference. What is new across the set is the treatment of validation, traceability, and computational throughput as explicit software concerns, delivered as open tools.",
